site_name.description:
- Network: {network}
- NMI ending {nmi}
- Active from: {active_from}

Give the site a friendly name. It names the site's device; entity IDs don't depend on it. To rename it later, use **Rename site** in the site's settings; areas and labels are set on the device's page.

allowance.description:
Some networks charge for exports in a midday window, but only beyond a free allowance applied on the bill (for example Endeavour Energy's N61: 8 kWh a day, over the billing period). Amber's usage data charges every exported kWh in that window, so feed-in earnings look lower than on the bill. With this on, the integration measures the charge from your data, refunds it within the allowance in an extra "adjusted compensation" statistic, and uses it in the bill estimate.

{detected}

Billing day: {billing_day} (needed to total over the billing period).

channels.description:
Amber now reports different metering channels for this site.

New: {added}
No longer reported: {removed}

New channels get statistics from the next day to be imported. Statistics of channels that are no longer reported are kept, but no longer updated.

legacy_cleanup.description:
The migration is complete. Remove these leftovers of the YAML kit when you are satisfied:

{items}

This notice only lists them; nothing here is removed automatically. It is re-checked after each scheduled run and when Home Assistant starts, and clears itself once they are all gone. The old statistics are kept until you delete them with the delete_legacy_statistics action; see the migration guide.

add.description:
The Energy dashboard has no grid connection yet. Submit to add this site's statistics:

{sources}

A copy of the current Energy dashboard settings is saved first.

other.description:
The Energy dashboard has a grid connection that doesn't use this integration's statistics, so it was left unchanged. To use them, edit the grid connection in **Settings > Dashboards > Energy** and choose:

{sources}

Submit to dismiss this notice if you prefer to keep your current setup; it won't be shown again for this site.

plan.description:
Version 2.2 replaces own energy sensors with two kinds of mapping:

- a **grid meter**: a meter on your main supply (import, and optionally export), compared each day with Amber's billed totals;
- an **appliance**: any other energy sensor, priced but not compared.

Your own energy sensors:

{mappings}

Choose which of them make your grid meter. Every other one becomes an appliance. Cost statistics and their history are kept.

confirm.description:
These replace your own energy sensors:

{plan}

Each sensor's cost statistic and its history are kept. The old own-sensor devices and their reconciliation sensors are removed; a grid meter's reconciliation sensors take over their entity IDs. Appliances have yesterday's energy and cost instead of reconciliation. The integration reloads afterwards.

